  "narrative": "During the 2020s, Black and African women's hairstyles and hair culture continue to thrive, building upon the rich traditions of their ancestors. The natural hair movement, which originated in the 1960s and gained momentum in the 2000s, remains a powerful force, encouraging individuals to celebrate and care for their afro-textured locks. Hairstyles such as box braids, cornrows, and dreadlocks are still prominent, offering a range of protective styles that not only preserve hair health but also serve as a testament to cultural heritage. The Jheri curl, a nostalgic reminder of the 1980s, has largely faded from popularity, while the afro continues to be a symbol of pride and identity for many women. Despite the limitations in documentation specific to this decade, it is clear that Black and African women's hairstyles remain an integral part of their cultural expression and a vital aspect of their identities.",
